For a manic or hypomanic episode to be counted toward a diagnosis of bipolar and related disorder due to another medical condition, the episode must:

- be attributable to another medical condition (i.e., the mood and activity changes are due to the medical condition) [Psichiatry_DSM-5_755];  
- cause clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning [Psichiatry_DSM-5_756] (Criterion E);  
- not occur exclusively during a delirium [Psichiatry_DSM-5_756] (Criterion D); and  
- not have clearly preceded the onset of the medical condition (unless the unusual circumstance applies where all prior episodes were substance/medication‑related) [Psichiatry_DSM-5_755][Psichiatry_DSM-5_756].

This is educational information, not medical advice.
